  <!-- OVERVIEW -->
  <section class="section" id="overview" aria-label="Overview">
    <h1 class="display">Overview</h1>
    <div class="panel panel--warm">
      <div class="split split--aside">
        <div>
          <p class="section__eyebrow">The problem</p>
          <p class="body">CS reps spent days preparing just to decide which customers needed outreach, and in what order. Churn risk often hid in conversations other reps had with the account, even while the usage data looked healthy. And after every connected call, reps lost hours to the follow-up, because not every customer question could be answered live and most issues couldn't be resolved on the spot.</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <button class="zoom" aria-label="Open the system architecture diagram larger">
            <span class="frame"><img src="img/proactive_ai_vision/simplified-system-architecture.png" alt="Simplified system architecture: a context layer feeding an orchestrator that triggers the outreach, meeting, live-call and success agents"></span>
            <span class="zoom__hint"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 9V4h5M15 4h5v5M20 15v5h-5M9 20H4v-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></span>
          </button>
          <figcaption class="figcaption"><span class="figcaption__n">Fig 1:</span> A simplified architecture of the system.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="section__eyebrow">The approach</p>
      <p class="lead">The Success Assistant already let reps ask the AI anything and get a useful answer, but it was <strong>reactive</strong>; it waited to be asked. This vision makes Customer Success <strong>proactive</strong>: a system that catches risk and opportunity before a rep would, prepares the response as reviewable drafts, and leaves every decision with the rep. It works as one arc:</p>
      <div class="steps steps--row">
        <div class="steps__item">
          <h3 class="steps__title"><span class="steps__n">1</span>Prioritize the book</h3>
          <p class="steps__body">The Orchestrator is the proactive engine, not an agent reps talk to. It continuously reads the context layer and the rep's whole book for the two signals humans miss: accounts sliding toward churn while the usage still looks healthy, and accounts ready to grow. It ranks who needs the rep today, shows why, and sets the downstream agents to work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="steps__item">
          <h3 class="steps__title"><span class="steps__n">2</span>Reach out</h3>
          <p class="steps__body">The Outreach agent drafts proactive, personalized outreach in the rep's voice, timed to that account's moment. The rep reviews, adjusts, and sends. The agent writes; the rep decides.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="steps__item">
          <h3 class="steps__title"><span class="steps__n">3</span>Meet and follow-up</h3>
          <p class="steps__body">The Meeting agent drafts the prep before the call and the recap after, while the Live call assistant surfaces answers in the moment. Prep, live call, and recap flow as one experience, each a draft the rep confirms, and the recap feeds the next cycle so the system sharpens over time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DISCOVERY -->
  <section class="section" id="discover" aria-label="Discovery research">
    <h1>Discovery research</h1>
    <p class="body">I built the vision on the following three layers of evidence:</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="card__title">Synthesized existing research</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>I pulled every relevant past study, mine and other designers', into NotebookLM to surface recurring pain-point themes and map the rep's journeys.</li>
          <li>These journeys uncovered where automation will be useful.</li>
          <li>NotebookLM let cross-functional stakeholders ask deeper questions and validate the findings and direction.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag">Research synthesis</span><span class="tag">Journey mapping</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="card__title">Mined the Success Assistant's chat logs</h3>
        <p class="card__text">I had designed HubSpot CS's <strong>first conversational AI assistant</strong> before this vision. Using Claude, I analyzed its real chat logs against the pain themes, cross-matching to see what reps were actually trying to accomplish with proprietary HubSpot data behind the assistant.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag">Behavioural log analysis</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="card__title">Validated with CS reps</h3>
        <p class="card__text">I interviewed CS reps of varying tenures and geo-locations to pressure-test the themes and pinpoint where the real gaps were, grounding the vision in their reality.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag">In-depth interviews</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel--warm">
      <div class="insight">
        <div>
          <p class="lead">Reps were spending days deciding who to call, right as the bar for connected calls got higher.</p>
          <p class="insight__label">The core insight</p>
        </div>
        <div class="insight__body">
          <p>Reps spent a huge share of their time just prioritizing their book of business, manually combining:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Unified Usage Score (a health model I had designed earlier)</li>
            <li>Past conversations, literally reading through engagements (emails, call logs, notes)</li>
            <li>Subscription data</li>
            <li>Product updates that fit a particular customer need</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Success Assistant helped prioritize, draft outreach and prep a lot faster than before, but it still required reps to prompt it well. At that moment, most reps weren't AI fluent, and most of what they were trying to do could be automated and performed upfront for them to review.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DESIGNING THE SYSTEM -->
  <section class="section" id="design" aria-label="Designing the system end-to-end">
    <h1>Designing the system end-to-end</h1>
    <div class="media-row">
      <div class="media-row__text">
        <h2 class="media-row__lead">A completely redesigned CS Workspace, where reps begin their day without piecing together what to do next.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Top priorities are surfaced, ranked, and ready to go: no digging, no second-guessing.</li>
          <li>AI-prepared strategies, emails, and plans are waiting at their fingertips.</li>
          <li>Calendar, goals, and metrics all in one view, seamlessly connected.</li>
          <li>With the Success Assistant built in, the rep doesn't work alone. One click to shift focus, refine priorities, or ask it to spotlight something new.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag">Orchestrator agent</span></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="media-row__media">
        <button class="zoom" aria-label="Enlarge the CS Workspace walkthrough">
